  \nThus each sunny line can cover at most one of the three remaining points, forcing at least three sunny lines – a contradiction.\n\nIf \\(|L_0\\cap T_3|=2\\) (e.g. \\(L_0:x=2\\)), the set of uncovered points is larger, and the same argument (any two of them determine a non‑sunny line) again forces more than two sunny lines. Hence this case is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 3 – \\(L_0\\) is diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\))\n\nFor the points of \\(T_3\\) the possible sums \\(a+b\\) are \\(2,3,4,5,6\\).  \nThus a line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most the points with a common sum:\n\n* \\(c=2\\): \\((1,1)\\) (one point);\n* \\(c=3\\): \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) (two points);\n* \\(c=4\\): \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (three points);\n* \\(c\\ge5\\): no points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nHence \\(|L_0\\cap T_3|\\le 3\\).  \nIf \\(|L_0\\cap T_3|=3\\) we may take \\(L_0:x+y=4\\); the uncovered points are\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of these three points is respectively\n\n* vertical   \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\),\n* horizontal \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\),\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\),\n\nall non‑sunny. Consequently each sunny line can contain at most one of the three uncovered points, again requiring at least three sunny lines – a contradiction.\n\nIf \\(|L_0\\cap T_3|=2\\) (e.g. \\(x+y=3\\)) the set of uncovered points has size four, and the same reasoning shows that any two of them determine a non‑sunny line, so more than two sunny lines would be needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince all three possibilities for the unique non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction, **no configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_3\\).**\n\nTherefore, for \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=2\\) is **not attainable**. ∎"
