            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” becomes \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \nHence the set of required points is  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct **sunny** lines (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)) whose union contains all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Candidate sunny lines through pairs of points  \n\nCompute the slope of the line through each unordered pair of points of \\(T_3\\).  \nA slope is forbidden if it equals \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), is undefined (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), or equals \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nThe admissible pairs and their slopes are:\n\n| Pair of points | Slope | Equation |\n|----------------|-------|----------|\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(1\\) | \\(y=x\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(-\\dfrac12\\) | \\(y=-\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac52\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\(-2\\) | \\(y=-2x+5\\) |\n\nAll other pairs give a forbidden slope (vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\)).  \nEach of the three admissible lines contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Point‑by‑point verification  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|-----------------|----------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(2 = -\\frac12\\cdot1 + \\frac52 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(1 = -\\frac12\\cdot3 + \\frac52 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\\) ✓ |\n\nThus every point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Sunny property  \n\nThe slopes of \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\) are respectively \\(1,\\ -\\dfrac12,\\ -2\\).  \nNone equals \\(0\\), is undefined, or equals \\(-1\\); therefore each line is **sunny** by definition.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Distinctness  \n\nThe three equations are different, so the lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\nSince we have exhibited three distinct sunny lines covering all required points, a configuration with exactly \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=3\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
