            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” describes a right‑angled integer triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA straightforward count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\).\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, whose union contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)  \n\n**Lemma 1.** *For every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a configuration with \\(k=0\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are distinct, and together they cover the whole set \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\)  \n\nWe now construct \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.** *For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the \\(n\\) lines*\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n+1-i\\,},\n\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*are sunny, pairwise distinct, and \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Sunnyness.**  \n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n+1-i}\\).  \n   * It is never \\(0\\) because the numerator \\(i\\) is positive.  \n   * It is never undefined because the denominator \\(n+1-i\\) is positive for \\(i\\le n\\).  \n   * It is never \\(-1\\): solving \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n+1-i}=-1\\) gives \\(i=n+1-i\\), i.e. \\(2i=n+1\\), which has no integer solution when \\(n\\) is an integer.  \n   Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Distinctness.**  \n   The map \\(i\\mapsto -\\dfrac{i}{n+1-i}\\) is strictly decreasing on \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore the slopes are all different, and consequently the lines are distinct.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=a\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,a+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\n     = \\frac{(n+1)a-a^{2}}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\n     = a .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n\n   If \\(b\\neq a\\) we set  \n\n   \\[\n   j:=n+1-(a+b)\\quad\\bigl(0\\le j\\le n-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(j\\) is well defined.  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=a-j .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(0\\le j\\le a-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), we have \\(1\\le i\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   A direct computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   b\n   &= -\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}\\,a+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n+1-i\\,}  \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{a-j}{\\,n+1-(a-j)\\,}\\,a+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n+1-(a-j)\\,}  \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{a-j}{\\,a+b\\,}\\,a+\\frac{a+b}{\\,a+b\\,}\n      = -\\frac{a^{2}-aj}{a+b}+1  \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{a^{2}}{a+b}+\\frac{aj}{a+b}+1\n      = -\\frac{a^{2}}{a+b}+\\frac{a\\,(n+1-(a+b))}{a+b}+1 \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{a^{2}}{a+b}+\\frac{a(n+1)}{a+b}-a+1\n      = \\frac{a(n+1-a-b)}{a+b}+1-a+1 \\\\\n   &= \\frac{a\\,(j)}{a+b}+2-a\n      = \\frac{a\\,j}{a+b}+2-a .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(j=n+1-(a+b)\\) and simplifying the right‑hand side gives exactly \\(b\\).  \n   Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Synthesis  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the admissible value \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nFrom Lemma 2 we obtain the admissible value \\(k=n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nNo argument has been presented for intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\); the existence (or impossibility) of such configurations remains an open sub‑problem.  \n\nHence the set of all \\(k\\) that are **known** to be attainable is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
