"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma 1 (odd\u2011\\(k\\) construction).**  \n   For every odd integer \\(s\\;(1\\le s\\le n)\\) there exists a family of exactly \\(s\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_s\\).  \n   The construction is explicit: for \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{\\,s+1-i\\,}\\,x+\\frac{s+1}{\\,s+1-i\\,}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of Lemma\u202f1* \u2013 we verify that each point \\((a,b)\\in T_s\\) lies on one of the lines (1).  \n   If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) in (1) gives \\(y=a\\).  \n   If \\(a\\neq b\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=\\frac{(s+1)(1-b)}{a-b}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le s+1\\), the right\u2013hand side of (2) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(s\\); moreover (2) is exactly the solution of the linear equation obtained by inserting \\((a,b)\\) into (1).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\) and belongs to that line.  Thus every point of \\(T_s\\) is covered.  \n   The slopes \\(-\\dfrac{i}{s+1-i}\\) are never \\(0\\), never undefined and never \\(-1\\) (the equality \\(-\\dfrac{i}{s+1-i}=-1\\) would give \\(2i=s+1\\), impossible because \\(s\\) is odd), so all the lines are sunny and distinct.  \u220e  \n\n3. **Key Lemma 2 (extension to any \\(n\\)).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an odd integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Write \\(n=k+r\\) with \\(r\\ge 0\\).  \n   * Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f1 (with \\(s=k\\)) to cover the set  \n\n     \\[\n     T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   * For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) add the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n     These \\(r=n-k\\) vertical lines are pairwise distinct, are **not** sunny, and each of them covers all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\).  \n\n   Because the two families are disjoint in the set of lines, we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to the vertical part (if its first coordinate exceeds \\(k\\)) or to the sunny part (otherwise).  Hence a configuration with any prescribed odd \\(k\\) exists.\n\n4. **Key Lemma 3 (the case \\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n5. **Key Lemma 4 (even \\(k>0\\) is impossible).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly an even positive number \\(k\\) of sunny lines exists.  \n   Let \\(H_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) be the set of points on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   *Every sunny line meets the hypotenuse in at most one point* (otherwise two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) would lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunnyness).  \n   *Every non\u2011sunny line meets the hypotenuse in at most one point* as well (vertical lines meet it at a single point, horizontal lines likewise, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the hypotenuse and would then be non\u2011sunny, but such a line would contain **all** points of \\(H_n\\) and consequently could not be distinct from the other \\(n-1\\) lines).  \n\n   Hence the total number of distinct points of \\(H_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family of \\(n\\) lines is