\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Covering a triangle with an odd number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every odd integer }s\\;(1\\le s\\le n)\\text{ there exist }s\n\\text{ distinct sunny lines covering }T_s .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,s\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{\\,s+1-i\\,}\\,x+\\frac{s+1}{\\,s+1-i\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof that the lines are sunny and distinct.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i}{s+1-i}\\).  \nBecause \\(i>0\\) the slope is never \\(0\\); because \\(s+1-i>0\\) it is never\n\\(\\infty\\); and \\(-\\dfrac{i}{s+1-i}=-1\\) would give \\(2i=s+1\\), impossible\nwhen \\(s\\) is odd.  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe function \\(i\\mapsto -\\dfrac{i}{s+1-i}\\) is strictly decreasing on\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,s\\}\\); therefore the slopes are all different and the lines are\npairwise distinct.\n\n*Proof that the lines cover \\(T_s\\).*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_s\\).\n\n*Case \\(a=b\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{a}{s+1-a}\\,a+\\frac{s+1}{s+1-a}\n   =\\frac{(s+1)a-a^{2}}{s+1-a}=a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*Case \\(a\\neq b\\).*  \nInsert \\((a,b)\\) into (1) and solve for \\(i\\):\n\n\\[\nb=-\\frac{i}{s+1-i}\\,a+\\frac{s+1}{s+1-i}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\ni(a-b)=(s+1)(1-b).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ni=\\frac{(s+1)(1-b)}{a-b}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le s+1\\), the numerator\n\\((s+1)(1-b)\\) is a non\u2011negative multiple of \\(s+1\\) and the denominator\n\\(a-b\\) divides it; consequently the right\u2011hand side of (2) is a positive\ninteger not exceeding \\(s\\).  Denote this integer by \\(i\\).  By construction\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_s\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_s\\).\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  From a triangle of size \\(k\\) to the original triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n,\\text{ then there exists a family\nof }n\\text{ distinct lines containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines and\ncovering }T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(s=k\\); we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=c .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are non\u2011sunny, are pairwise distinct, and each\ncovers precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\).\nSince the two families involve disjoint sets of points, together they cover\nevery element of \\(T_n\\).  The total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=0\\)  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{The }n\\text{ vertical lines }x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\text{ cover }T_n\n\\text{ and contain no sunny line.}\n\\]\n\nThe statement is immediate from the definition of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Even positive numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 4.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }k>0\\text{ is even, no configuration of }n\\text{ distinct lines\nwith exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines can cover }T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exist and denote the whole\nfamily by \\(\\mathcal L\\) (\\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\)).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Claim 1.*  A sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(H_n\\).  \nIndeed, two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); a line of slope\n\\(-1\\) is not sunny.\n\n*Claim 2.*  A non\u2011sunny line also contains at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the hypotenuse at \\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal\nline \\(y=c\\) meets it at \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with\nthe whole hypotenuse, but then it would be non\u2011sunny **and** would be the\nonly line of that slope, leaving at most \\(n-1\\) further distinct lines to\ncover the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(H_n\\); this forces the remaining lines\nto be vertical or horizontal, which would duplicate intersection points \u2013\nimpossible because the points of \\(H_n\\) are distinct.  Hence a slope\n\\(-1\\) line cannot appear in a covering of all points.\n\nConsequently each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets \\(H_n\\) in exactly one point,\nand the set of intersection points is precisely \\(H_n\\).  Therefore the\nnon\u2011sunny lines consist only of vertical and horizontal lines, and their\nnumbers satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\}=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nbecause the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto (y,x)\\) sends a vertical line intersecting\n\\((i,n+1-i)\\) to the horizontal line intersecting \\((n+1-i,i)\\).\n\nThe total number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-k\\).  Since \\(k\\) is even,\n\\(n-k\\) has the **same parity as \\(n\\)**.  In particular \\(n-k\\) is odd\n(because \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) have opposite parity).  An odd integer cannot be\nwritten as the sum of two equal integers, contradicting (3).  Hence the\nassumption that a configuration with an even positive \\(k\\) exists is false. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\n*Existence.*  \n- \\(k=0\\) is possible by Lemma\u202f3.  \n- If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), Lemma\u202f2 provides a configuration with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non\u2011existence.*  \n- Lemma\u202f4 shows that no even \\(k>0\\) can occur.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is completely solved.**"

