r negative. The argument that the numerator is a non\u2011negative multiple of \\(s+1\\) is false in general. Moreover, even when the numerator is negative, the fraction could still be an integer, but the sign analysis is mishandled. The statement that the denominator \\(a-b\\) always divides the numerator is not justified; divisibility does not follow from the given inequalities.  \n\n  **Finding:** Critical Error \u2013 the reasoning that (2) yields a valid integer \\(i\\) for every \\((a,b)\\in T_s\\) is flawed, so the claim that every off\u2011diagonal point lies on some \\(L_i\\) is unsupported.\n\n#### Conclusion of Lemma\u202f1  \n\nBecause the construction includes a non\u2011sunny line and the coverage argument for off\u2011diagonal points is invalid, Lemma\u202f1 is false. This constitutes a **Critical Error** that undermines all later results that depend on Lemma\u202f1.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n**Claim:** \u201cIf \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), then there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Proof Sketch:** \u201cApply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(s=k\\) \u2026 For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) add the vertical line \\(x=c\\). \u2026 Since the two families involve disjoint sets of points, together they cover every element of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n#### Dependency on Lemma\u202f1  \n\nSince Lemma\u202f1 is false, the existence of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\) is not established. Hence the construction in Lemma\u202f2 lacks a valid foundation. This is a **Critical Error** propagated from Lemma\u202f1.\n\n#### Disjointness Assertion  \n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cSince the two families involve disjoint sets of points \u2026\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** No argument is provided that the points covered by the sunny lines (which are supposed to cover \\(T_k\\)) are disjoint from those covered by the vertical lines \\(x=c\\) for \\(c>k\\). While it may be tr