ct: each meets \\(H_n\\) in exactly one point.  \n  However, the statement that \u201ca line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the whole hypotenuse\u201d is true, but such a line **does** contain *all* points of \\(H_n\\), contradicting the claim that a non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point of \\(H_n\\). The subsequent argument attempts to rule out using a slope \\(-1\\) line, but it is not rigorous: it assumes that using a slope \\(-1\\) line would force the remaining lines to duplicate intersection points, but this is not demonstrated. In fact, a configuration could include the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny) together with other lines that cover the interior points without any duplication. Therefore Claim\u202f2 is **false** as stated.  \n\n  **Finding:** Critical Error \u2013 the assertion that every non\u2011sunny line meets \\(H_n\\) in at most one point is incorrect.\n\n#### Parity Argument  \n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cSince \\(k\\) is even, \\(n-k\\) has the **same parity as \\(n\\)**.  In particular \\(n-k\\) is odd (because \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) have opposite parity).\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** The two sentences contradict each other. If \\(k\\) is even, then \\(n-k\\) indeed has the same parity as \\(n\\). Whether \\(n-k\\) is odd depends on the parity of \\(n\\); it is odd only when \\(n\\) is odd. The claim \u201c\\(n-k\\) is odd because \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) have opposite parity\u201d is false when both \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) are even. This logical inconsistency invalidates the subsequent conclusion that an odd number cannot be expressed as the sum of two equal integers.  \n\n  **Finding:** Critical Error \u2013 the parity reasoning is incorrect and leads to an unfounded contradiction.\n\n#### Overall Conclusion of Lemma\u202f4  \n\nBecause Claim\u202f2 is false and the parity argument is flawed, the deduction that even \\(k>0\\) is impossible is unsupported. This constitutes a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Final Determination (Section\u202f5)\n