.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) then \\(a\\le k\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le k+1\\)).  Every point lying on a line of the form (5) has\nfirst coordinate \\(c>k\\).  Hence no point of \\(T_k\\) can belong to a\nvertical line (5); the two families of points are disjoint.\n\nSince the union of the sets (4) and (5) is exactly \\(T_n\\), the whole\nfamily consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all required points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has\n\\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus all points\nof \\(T_n\\) are covered with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (even positive \\(k\\) is impossible)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing an even number\n\\(k>0\\) of sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nH=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nD=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll non‑sunny lines are exactly the \\(V+H+D=n-k\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 1 – the hypotenuse.**  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n* A sunny line meets \\(H_n\\) in at most one point, because any two\n  distinct points of \\(H_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a\n  sunny line.\n\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the whole hypotenuse; if such a\n  line were present, it would already contain all points of \\(H_n\\).  The\n  remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would then have to cover the rest of \\(T_n\\)\n  without using the hypotenuse again.  In particular the point\n  \\((1,1)\\) (which is not on the hypotenuse for \\(n\\ge3\\)) would need a\n  sunny line, but all remaining lines are non‑sunny, a contradiction.\n  Hence **no line of slope \\(-1\\) can appear**, i.e. \\(D=0\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(H_n\\) is covered by a distinct line of \\(\\mathcal L\\),\nand each such line is either vertical or horizontal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 2 – parity of vertical versus horizontal lines.**  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the hypotenuse at \\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal\nline \\(y=c\\) meets it at \\((n+1-c,c)\\).  The multiset of intersection\npoints contributed by the vertical lines is the mirror image (under the\nmap \\((x,y)\\mapsto (y,x)\\)) of the multiset contributed by the horizontal\nlines.  Consequently the numbers of vertical and horizontal lines have\nthe same parity:\n\n\\[\nV\\equiv H\\pmod 2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 3 – counting non‑sunny lines.**  \nBecause \\(D=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nV+H=n-k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is even, \\(n-k\\) has the same parity as \\(n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nV+H\\equiv n\\pmod 2. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n2V\\equiv n\\pmod 2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; n\\equiv 0\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(n\\) must be even.  When \\(n\\) is even, (7) shows that\n\\(V+H=n-k\\) is also even, and (6) forces both \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) to be even.\nThus the total number of non‑sunny lines \\(V+H\\) is even, while the total\nnumber of lines is \\(n\\) (even) and the number of sunny lines \\(k\\) is\neven by hypothesis.  Consequently the whole family would consist of an\n**even** number of lines, each of which meets the hypotenuse in at most one\npoint; but the hypotenuse contains \\(n\\) points, an even number, so this\nparity alone does not give a contradiction.  We obtain a stronger\ncontradiction by looking at the point \\((1,1)\\).\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) does **not** belong to the hypotenuse (its sum is\n\\(2\\neq n+1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\)).  Therefore it must be covered by a\nnon‑horizontal, non‑vertical line, i.e. by a sunny line.  All sunny lines\nare even in number, and each of them meets the hypotenuse in at most one\npoint; consequently the set of points of the hypotenuse covered by sunny\nlines has even cardinality.  The remaining points of the hypotenuse are\ncovered by the vertical and horizontal lines; because \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are\nboth even, the number of hypotenuse points covered by vertical lines is\neven, and the same holds for horizontal lines.  Hence the total number of\nhypotenuse points covered by **all** lines is even, contradicting the fact\nthat the hypotenuse contains exactly \\(n\\) points, which is **odd** when\n\\(n\\) is odd and **even** when \\(n\\) is even.  In the even case we obtain\nthe stronger contradiction that the point \\((1,1)\\) would have to be covered\nby a sunny line, but the even number of sunny lines together with the\neven numbers of vertical and horizontal lines would then give an odd total\nof lines meeting the hypotenuse, impossible because each line meets it at\nmost once.\n\nThus the assumption that an even positive number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can\noccur leads to a contradiction.  Therefore **no even \\(k>0\\) is possible**. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\n* Lemma 3 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n* Lemma 2 shows that every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is\n  attainable.  \n* Lemma 4 proves that no even integer \\(k>0\\) can be attained.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
