gether contain exactly six points, so we must have  \n   \\[\n   3s\\ge 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, any two points that lie in the **same** column have the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate; a sunny line cannot contain two such points (otherwise the line would be vertical).  \n   Therefore each column \\(a\\) of \\(T_n\\) needs either the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a distinct sunny line for **every** point of that column.\n\n   In particular the first column \\(C_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) contains \\(n\\) points.  \n   If the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is absent, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines to cover those \\(n\\) points, contradicting \\(s\\le n\\).  \n   Hence the line \\(x=1\\) must belong to \\(V\\); consequently \\(v\\ge 1\\) and\n\n   \\[\n   s=n-v\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (1) and (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le s\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Now look at the **second** column \\(C_{2}=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\).  \n   If the vertical line \\(x=2\\) were missing, each of its \\(n-1\\) points would have to be covered by a different sunny line (again because a sunny line cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate).  \n   That would require at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(s\\ge n-1\\).  \n   Together with (2) this forces \\(s=n-1\\) and consequently \\(v=1\\).\n\n   Thus **any** admissible configuration must contain exactly one vertical line, namely \\(x=1\\); all the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Hence the only possible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Eliminating the case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k=n-1\\) (so the unique non‑sunny line is \\(x=1\\)).  \n   The points of the second column \\(C_{2}\\) are \\((2,1),\\dots ,(2,n-1)\\).  \n   As argued above, a sunny line can contain at most one of them; therefore we would need at least \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines to cover the whole column \\(C_{2}\\).  \n   But we have exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, so each of them must pass through a *different* point of \\(C_{2}\\).  \n   Consequently every sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in a point distinct from \\((1,1)\\); hence each sunny line is forced to have slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{b_i-1}{2-1}=b_i-1\\qquad (b_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. an **integer** slope \\(\\ge 0\\).  \n   The only integer slopes that are *forbidden* for a sunny line are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the only possible slopes are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   In particular, none of the sunny lines can have slope \\(-1\\) or be horizontal, and none can be vertical.  \n\n   Consider the three right‑most columns again.  \n   The point \\((n,1)\\) must lie on a sunny line; its slope is therefore \\(m=\\frac{1-1}{n-1}=0\\), which is forbidden.  \n   This contradiction shows that a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible values are those listed in (3) that survive the elimination step, namely  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\);  \n   * \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) (the construction of Step 2 provides it).  \n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the construction with one sunny line would require a sunny line through \\((3,1)\\); any line through that point and a point of the first column has slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), hence is not sunny.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ for all }n\\ge 3,\\qquad \nk=1\\text{ for all }n\\ge 4\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two explicit families\n\n*All lines non‑sunny.*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Exactly one sunny line (possible when \\(n\\ge4\\)).*  \nKeep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny) and add the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=(n-1)x-(n-2)n .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S\\) equals \\(n-1\\neq0,-1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny.  \nSince the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) and \\(S\\) satisfies \\(1=(n-1)n-(n-2)n\\), the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and the family contains exactly one sunny line.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary‑condition lemma\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{L\\in\\mathcal F\\mid L\\text{ is vertical}\\},\\qquad \nv=|V|,\\qquad s=n-v\\;(= \\text{number of sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(v\\ge 1\\) and the line \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(V\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nColumn \\(C_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) contains \\(n\\) points.  \nIf \\(x=1\\notin V\\), each of those \\(n\\) points would have to lie on a distinct sunny line (a sunny line cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate).  That would require at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(s\\ge n\\), contradicting \\(s=n-v\\le n\\).  Hence \\(x=1\\in V\\) and \\(v\\ge1\\). ∎\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIf \\(x=2\\notin V\\) then \\(s\\ge n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nColumn \\(C_{2}=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\) has \\(n-1\\) points.  \nAs before, a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C_{2}\\); therefore covering \\(C_{2}\\) without the vertical line \\(x=2\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, i.e. \\(s\\ge n-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 we have \\(v\\ge1\\); from Lemma 2.2, if \\(v\\ge2\\) then \\(s\\le n-2\\).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(s\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge2\\)).  \nBecause \\(v=n-s\\le n-2\\), the vertical lines that are present are a subset of \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\}\\).  \nConsequently the three right‑most columns  \n\n\\[\nC_{n}=\\{(n,1)\\},\\;\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\;\nC_{n-2}=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontain **six** points, none of which lies on a vertical line (because the only vertical lines we have are \\(x\\le n-2\\)).  \n\nA sunny line meets each vertical line in at most one point, hence it can meet the three columns in at most three points (at most one from each column).  With \\(s\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(3s\\) of the six points.  \nThus we need \\(3s\\ge6\\), i.e. \\(s\\ge2\\); this is already satisfied, but we obtain a sharper restriction by looking at the **first** column.\n\nThe first column \\(C_{1}\\) contains \\(n\\) points and is covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nAll remaining columns \\(C_{2},C_{3},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) each contain at least two points.  \nSince a sunny line can contain at most one point from any given column, covering all points of the columns \\(C_{2},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) would require at least  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=2}^{n-2} (n+1-a) = \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\ndifferent sunny lines.  Because \\(s=n-v\\le n-1\\), this is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\) unless \\(s\\le1\\).\n\nHence for \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have \\(s\\le1\\); together with Lemma 2.1 (which guarantees at least one vertical line) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1\\}\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nIf we tried to use one sunny line, the only way to keep a vertical line is \\(x=1\\); the remaining point \\((2,1)\\) would have to lie on a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) or \\((1,2)\\).  \nBoth possibilities give a line of slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), which are not sunny.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence for the admissible values\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the family of vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (Section 1).  \n* \\(k=1\\) (only for \\(n\\ge4\\)): the family consisting of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny=(n-1)x-(n-2)n ,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the last column (Section 1).\n\nBoth families consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines and satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ is always possible; }k=1\\text{ is possible exactly when }n\\ge4.\\;}\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur."
