   \\]\n   pass respectively through the pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n   Together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility for the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration satisfying the covering condition contain  \n\n   * \\(v\\) vertical lines,\n   * \\(h\\) horizontal lines,\n   * \\(d\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\),\n   * \\(s\\) sunny lines,  \n\n   with \\(v+h+d+s=n\\) and \\(k=s\\).\n\n   Consider the three right\u2011most columns of the triangle:\n   \\[\n   C_{n}=\\{(n,1)\\},\\qquad\n   C_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\n   C_{n-2}=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}.\n   \\]\n   They contain exactly six points.\n\n   *Each vertical line can cover at most the points of a single column, so at most three of the six points can be covered by vertical lines (one from each of the three columns).*\n\n   *Each horizontal line meets each of the three columns in at most one point, and the same holds for each line of slope \\(-1\\). Hence the total number of points of the three columns that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny, non\u2011vertical lines is at most \\(2\\,(n-v)\\).*\n\n   *A sunny line meets each of the three columns in at most one point, so the six points can be covered by the sunny lines in at most \\(3s\\) positions.*\n\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   6\\;\\le\\;3\\;(\\text{vertical covered})\\;+\\;2\\,(n-v)\\;+\\;3s .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(v=n-s-h-d\\le n-s\\), we have \\(n-v\\ge s\\). Substituting this lower bound into (5.1) gives\n   \\[\n   6\\;\\le\\;3\\;+\\;2s\\;+\\;3s\\;=\\;3\\;+\\;5s,\n   \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad s\\ge\\frac{3}{5}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(s\\) is an integer, either \\(s=0\\) or \\(s\\ge1\\).\n\n   Assume now that \\(2\\le s\\le n-2\\).  \n   From (5.1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   6\\;\\le\\;3\\;+\\;2\\,(n-v)\\;+\\;3s\n   \\;\\le\\;3\\;+\\;2\\,(n-(n-s))\\;+\\;3s\n   \\;=\\;3\\;+\\;2s\\;+\\;3s\n   \\;=\\;