t‑most column and through no other point of \\(T_n\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines and exactly one of them (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1&:\\;y=x,\\\\\n   \\ell_2&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n   \\ell_3&:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   contain respectively the pairs  \n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\quad\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\quad\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\n   \\]\n   and together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\). Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(n\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   *Each sunny line can intersect the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) lattice points* (a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can meet the set \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most that many integer points).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\n   \\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4,\n   \\]\n   a contradiction. Consequently \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Open part.**  \n   The existence (or non‑existence) of a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines for arbitrary \\(n\\ge3\\) has not been settled in the present work.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The values }k=0,\\;k=1\\text{ are possible for all }n\\ge3,\\;\n       k=n\\text{ only for }n=3,\\;\n       \\text{and no other }k\\text{ is known to be possible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified i