igl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|\\beta|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\)\n   admissible values of \\(t\\).  \n   The condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) together with \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\neq0\\) forces\n   \\(|\\beta|\\ge 1\\) and yields the sharper estimate  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\text{ points of }T_n.}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The proof of (1) is carried out by a case‑by‑case analysis of the sign of\n   \\(\\alpha\\) (the case \\(|\\beta|>1\\) gives a still smaller bound, while the\n   extremal case \\(|\\beta|=1\\) gives exactly \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Step 1.* Choose \\(k\\) distinct columns among the first \\(n-1\\) columns,\n   i.e. choose distinct integers \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\) with \\(1\\le c_i\\le n-1\\).  \n   *Step 2.* In each chosen column pick an arbitrary lattice point\n   \\((c_i,d_i)\\in T_n\\) (such a point always exists because the column\n   \\(c_i\\) contains at least two points).  \n   *Step 3.* Through each \\((c_i,d_i)\\) draw a line of **irrational**\n   slope; such a line meets the integer lattice only at the chosen point,\n   hence it is sunny and covers exactly that one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Step 4.* The remaining columns are precisely the \\(n-k\\) columns\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\).  For each of them draw the\n   vertical line \\(x=j\\); vertical lines are non‑sunny.  Every point of the\n   remaining columns lies on its column‑line, so all points of \\(T_n\\) are\n   covered.\n\n   The \\(k\\) irrational‑slope lines are distinct (different slopes) and are\n   sunny; the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are distinct and non‑sunny.  Hence we have\n   produced a configuration of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for **every**\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Assume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  By (1) each of them contains at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Moreover,\n   two distinct sunny lines cannot share a point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise that\n   point would belong to two different lines, contradicting the definition of\n   a *cover*).  Consequently the total number of distinct points that can be\n   covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) with strict\n   inequality for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  Hence (2) is strictly smaller than the\n   required number of points, a contradiction.  Therefore a configuration\n   with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5,\n   \\]\n\n   which contain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\),\n   \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\).  Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable\n   for \\(n=3\\).\n\nCombining the constructions of §3 with the impossibility proved in §4 we\nobtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nK_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
